Abstract

This document defines the dynamic behavior of cognition within the Memory Operating System (MOS).

Where RFC-0001 specifies the structure of cognition, this RFC specifies how cognition evolves over time.

It introduces the concepts of Cognitive Forces, Cognitive Policies, Cognitive Constraints, Activation, Attention, State Transitions, Reflection, Learning, Prediction, and Equilibrium.

These concepts define the "physics" governing the Cognitive State Space.

1. Purpose

The purpose of this specification is to define how Cognitive Elements change while preserving the invariants established by RFC-0001.

This RFC defines the dynamic semantics of MOS independently of any implementation.

Workers, language models, databases, APIs, and reasoning engines SHALL conform to these dynamics regardless of implementation.

2. The Dynamics Axiom

MOS defines cognition as continuous evolution.

A Memory Operating System SHALL evolve its Cognitive State Space through the application of Cognitive Forces acting upon Cognitive Elements under Cognitive Constraints according to Cognitive Policies.

This is the primary axiom of Cognitive Dynamics.

3. Cognitive Dynamics Model

Every cognitive change SHALL be represented using the following model.

Current State

↓

Force

↓

Constraints

↓

Policies

↓

Transition

↓

New State

↓

Equilibrium

This model applies to every Cognitive Element.

No exception exists.

4. Cognitive Forces

A Cognitive Force represents any influence capable of modifying the Cognitive State Space.

Forces do not directly perform work.

Instead, they define categories of cognitive transformation.

Workers, language models, humans, sensors, and external systems implement forces.

Properties

Every Cognitive Force SHALL possess:

Identity
Type
Priority
Provenance
Scope
Inputs
Outputs
Preconditions
Postconditions
Standard Force Categories

MOS defines the following standard force classes.

Observation Force

Introduces new observations.

Interpretation Force

Transforms observations into candidate meanings.

Validation Force

Evaluates supporting evidence.

Reasoning Force

Generates new cognitive relationships.

Attention Force

Determines computational focus.

Planning Force

Generates or modifies goals and plans.

Execution Force

Records actions performed.

Reflection Force

Improves cognitive organization.

Learning Force

Modifies future behavior.

Prediction Force

Produces hypotheses regarding future states.

Consolidation Force

Compresses and reorganizes knowledge.

Decay Force

Reduces activation of unused cognition.

Correction Force

Repairs inconsistent cognition.

Security Force

Applies authorization policies.

Policy Force

Updates governing policies.

Future versions MAY introduce additional forces.

5. Cognitive Constraints

Constraints define what transitions are permitted.

Constraints SHALL be evaluated before any transition occurs.

Examples include:

Authorization
Security
Policy compliance
Temporal validity
Resource limits
Human approval
Contradiction limits
Ethical restrictions
Dependency requirements
Context limitations

A force SHALL NOT violate an active constraint.

6. Cognitive Policies

Policies determine preferred behavior.

Policies SHALL guide transitions but SHALL NOT override constraints.

Policies are Cognitive Elements.

Policies SHALL therefore possess:

Identity
Version
Provenance
Confidence
Scope

Examples include:

Prefer recent evidence.

Prioritize active goals.

Retain critical procedures.

Favor verified observations.

Require multiple evidence sources.

Policies MAY evolve through learning.

7. Attention

Attention is the allocation of computational resources within the Cognitive State Space.

Attention determines which Cognitive Elements become active.

Attention SHALL be explicit.

Attention SHALL NOT be inferred solely from retrieval mechanisms.

Attention is dynamic.

Attention is limited.

Attention is influenced by goals, context, novelty, urgency, importance, confidence, and policy.

8. Activation

Activation represents the current cognitive relevance of a Cognitive Element.

Activation SHALL be independent of storage.

Activation MAY vary continuously.

Activation SHALL be recalculated as cognition evolves.

Activation SHOULD consider:

Attention
Goal relevance
Confidence
Recency
Importance
Frequency
Novelty
User context
Environmental context
Policy

Implementations MAY include additional signals.

9. Working Memory

Working Memory is not persistent storage.

Working Memory is an activated projection of the Cognitive State Space.

Working Memory SHALL consist only of sufficiently activated Cognitive Elements.

Working Memory SHALL evolve continuously.

10. State Transitions

Every Cognitive Element SHALL transition through explicitly defined states.

Typical lifecycle:

Created

↓

Observed

↓

Interpreted

↓

Validated

↓

Linked

↓

Referenced

↓

Activated

↓

Strengthened

↓

Weakened

↓

Superseded

↓

Archived

Transitions SHALL preserve:

Identity
Provenance
Version history

Historical states SHALL remain reconstructable.

11. Cognitive Equilibrium

The Cognitive State Space continuously seeks equilibrium.

Equilibrium is achieved when no active force can produce additional valid transitions.

Equilibrium is temporary.

Any new observation MAY disrupt equilibrium.

12. Reflection

Reflection is the process of improving the organization of cognition.

Reflection SHALL NOT alter historical observations.

Reflection MAY:

Merge duplicate entities
Split ambiguous entities
Consolidate beliefs
Improve summaries
Extract procedures
Generate reusable skills
Detect contradictions
Recalculate confidence
Improve ontology
Strengthen relationships
Archive obsolete cognition

Reflection modifies structure.

It does not erase history.

13. Learning

Learning modifies future dynamics.

Learning SHALL preserve historical cognition.

Learning MAY modify:

Policies
Priorities
Confidence models
Activation models
Retrieval strategies
Planning strategies
Reflection strategies

Learning SHALL improve future cognition rather than rewrite the past.

14. Prediction

Prediction produces hypotheses regarding future states.

Predictions SHALL remain separate from observations.

Predictions SHALL possess confidence.

Future observations MAY validate or invalidate predictions.

Prediction SHALL therefore contribute to learning.

15. Planning

Planning creates intended future cognition.

Plans consist of:

Goals
Milestones
Tasks
Dependencies
Constraints
Expected outcomes

Planning SHALL remain versioned.

Planning SHALL adapt as cognition evolves.

16. Execution

Execution records interactions between cognition and external systems.

Execution SHALL preserve:

Inputs
Outputs
Context
Duration
Success
Failure
Side effects

Execution SHALL generate observations.

17. Passive and Active Cognition

MOS distinguishes between two cognitive modes.

Passive Cognition includes:
Observation
Storage
Retrieval
Recall

Active Cognition includes:

Reasoning
Planning
Prediction
Reflection
Learning
Decision-making

Both operate upon the same Cognitive State Space.

18. Continuous Optimization

MOS defines cognition as continuous optimization.

The objective is not maximum memory.

The objective is useful cognition.

Optimization SHALL balance:

Accuracy
Efficiency
Explainability
Adaptability
Stability
Resource utilization

No single objective dominates all others.

19. Compliance

An implementation SHALL preserve all dynamic semantics defined by this specification.

Implementation-specific optimizations SHALL NOT alter cognitive meaning.

